---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Panel from '../../components/Panel.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Multi-region DR rebuild — Somasuntaram">
  <Panel title="Overview">
    <h1>Multi-region disaster-recovery rebuild</h1>
    <p>
      A production Kubernetes platform ran in a single region, with no
      tested path to survive a regional failure. I designed and built a
      dedicated DR region, used the rebuild as the forcing function for a set of
      overdue version upgrades, and then proved it with an actual failover
      drill rather than calling it done once the Terraform applied cleanly.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <Panel title="Original design vs. DR design">
    <table class="fact-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Component</th><th>Original</th><th>DR design</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Availability zones</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Public subnets</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Private subnets</td><td>2</td><td>6 (3 in active use)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>NAT gateways</td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Route tables</td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A single shared NAT gateway is a single point of failure, so the DR
      design doesn't carry it forward. Three AZs, three NAT gateways, and
      per-AZ route tables were deliberate: a regional failover that still
      funnels every AZ's egress through one NAT gateway hasn't fixed the
      availability problem, only moved it.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <Panel title="Bundling the rebuild with overdue upgrades">
    <p>
      Standing up a second region meant building the cluster fresh anyway,
      so I used it to clear a version-upgrade backlog that would otherwise
      have needed its own separate, riskier in-place maintenance window:
    </p>
    <table class="fact-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Component</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Kubernetes</td><td>1.23</td><td>1.31</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Postgres (RDS)</td><td>13.13</td><td>16.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>API gateway</td><td>3.0</td><td>3.4 (LTS)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Node OS</td><td>Amazon Linux</td><td>Amazon Linux 2023</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jump-server OS</td><td>Ubuntu 20.04</td><td>Ubuntu 24.04</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Node.js runtime</td><td>16 / 18 / 20 (mixed)</td><td>22</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The same rebuild also modernised secrets management, which had nothing
      to do with regions specifically: application config moved from
      per-host <code>.env</code> files to AWS Secrets Manager pulled in via
      the Secrets Store CSI driver, and IAM access moved from static
      long-lived credentials to per-service-account roles via IRSA. Neither
      change depended on having two regions — they rode along because the
      cluster was being built from scratch either way, and retrofitting them
      onto the still-running primary later would have been strictly harder.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <Panel title="Node-group isolation">
    <p>
      The cluster runs five separate managed node groups instead of one
      general-purpose pool — gateway/edge, core request-serving services,
      compute processing, transaction handling, and a batch-style crawling
      workload — each isolated with its own taint so a pod only lands there
      if it explicitly tolerates that workload type. All five run on the
      same ARM-based instance family, so the isolation is entirely a
      scheduling decision, not a hardware one. Each group also gets its own
      autoscaling range sized to how bursty that workload is. The
      transaction-handling group scales the widest because its load is far
      less predictable than, say, the gateway tier's. A spike in one workload's resource usage scales
      that node group's autoscaler, not the whole cluster, and a bad deploy
      on the batch workload can't starve the gateway of capacity.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <Panel title="The drill" id="the-drill">
    <p>
      A DR region that's never been failed over to is a hypothesis, not a
      plan. I ran an actual drill against a staging dataset: simulate a
      primary-region database failure, promote the DR region, and measure
      what actually happens instead of what the runbook says should happen.
    </p>
    <div class="callout-good">
      <h3>What the drill actually showed</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>All three validation checks passed: DR-side availability,
          data-sync verification, and performance monitoring under the
          promoted database.</li>
        <li>Time from triggering the simulated failure to full restoration
          was measured and timestamped step by step, not estimated after the
          fact.</li>
        <li>Replication lag at the point of failover meant <strong>some recent
          writes were lost</strong>. That's the honest headline finding from
          the drill, and it set the target for the follow-up work.</li>
        <li>The drill covered database-level failover. DNS-level traffic
          failover is a separate exercise with its own drill.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p>
      I'd rather report the data loss than round the drill up to "DR
      verified." The follow-up work, reducing replication lag and automating
      replica promotion instead of running it by hand, is scoped precisely
      because the drill showed exactly where the gap was. Stopping at "the
      runbook completed without erroring" would have hidden it.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <Panel title="What I'd do differently">
    <p>
      Automate the failover instead of executing the runbook by hand. A
      human-paced failover shows the architecture works, but it isn't an RTO
      I'd commit to during a real incident. And I'd drill traffic failover
      as rigorously as database failover: a database that fails over cleanly
      doesn't help if traffic never reaches it.
    </p>
  </Panel>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .fact-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .fact-table th,
  .fact-table td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.75rem 0.4rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .fact-table th {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
